{
  "title": "iOS recipe sync failures are silent and the full sync is fragile",
  "description": "Follow-up to the fix that made browse sort/filter/clear \
apply from the local cache before the network sync. The underlying sync \
fragility remains:\n\
\n\
- A failed `GET /api/recipes/sync` is only written to the debug log. The user \
gets no signal that their cookbook is stale — new and edited recipes silently \
never arrive until some later sync happens to succeed. (Fail fast: this is \
exactly the kind of swallowed error the repo philosophy forbids.)\n\
- The first sync (and any sync after `lastSyncAt` is lost, e.g. the \
`cacheSchemaVersion` bump in #643 changed the UserDefaults key) transfers \
every recipe with full ingredients/instructions/notes in a single unpaginated \
response. For a large account on a slow or flaky connection this can time out \
repeatedly, and because `lastSyncAt` is only advanced on success, every retry \
is the same full payload. A permanently failing loop looks to the user like \
the pre-fix bugs: stale list, wasted bandwidth on every list operation.\n\
- While a slow sync is in flight, pull-to-refresh keeps the refresh spinner \
(which occupies the search-bar slot) until the sync resolves — reported as \
\"pull down and the search bar disappears and doesn't come back\".\n\
\n\
Scope:\n\
- Surface sync failures in the list UI (e.g. a small stale-data banner with \
retry), not just the debug log.\n\
- Paginate or chunk the full sync, or advance the cursor incrementally, so one \
giant response is never a prerequisite for a working cookbook.\n\
- Consider a timeout budget for the sync when it is only freshening an \
already-served list.\n\
- Web is unaffected (no local cache), but if the sync endpoint changes shape, \
regenerate clients and keep the iOS cache tests green.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 2,
  "type": "bug",
